From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:28:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 136/148] fix(polecat): close a re-pointed submit-and-exit step
 before drain-ack (gcp-fooz4)

Upstream #490 resolves the step to close with `gc hook current`, which
returns only the bead `gc hook --claim` last stamped on the session
(gc v1.5.0 cmd/gc/cmd_hook_current.go; stampHookSessionCurrentClaim,
cmd_hook_claim.go:1211). The fork holds the WORK bead in_progress for the
whole run (0060), so at a step boundary the hook's crash-recovery tier
serves and stamps the WORK bead, and the polecat startup block re-points
onto the real step with a plain `gc bd update <step> --claim`, which does
not move the stamp. At every submit-and-exit exit `gc hook current` then
names the WORK bead, the #490 guard refuses, and drain-ack never runs.
There is no gc CLI that sets the stamp to a named bead.

Each of the twelve exits keeps #490's resolve first. When `gc hook current`
fails or names a bead that is not an in-progress `*.submit-and-exit` step,
it falls back to this session's one in-progress step whose gc.step_ref ends
`.submit-and-exit`, listed by `--assignee="$BEADS_ACTOR"`. BEADS_ACTOR is
the claimant of that `--claim`: bd claims under $BEADS_ACTOR first (bd
1.3.0 `--actor` default), gc's bd passthrough leaves it in the child env
(cmd_bd.go bdCommandEnv -> mergeRuntimeEnv; FilterInherited strips only
secret-marker keys), and the session runtime env sets it to the same
AssigneeIdentifier the hook records claims under (internal/session/
lifecycle.go RuntimeEnvWithSessionContext). No BEADS_ACTOR, no match, or
more than one match refuses the ack with exit 1.

Also drops step 0's STEP_SESSION, dead since #490 replaced the step lookup.

Test: tests/test_v2_drain_ack_closes_own_step.py now runs every exit
against a fake gc. With the hook naming the WORK bead, or `gc hook current`
unavailable, all 24 cases were red against the unfixed formula
("current claim winnow-w1 is not this in-progress step; not acknowledging
drain") and are green now. #490's own path, and refusal with no own step,
two own steps, or no BEADS_ACTOR, stay green. The inference gate's
"halt close after escalation" anchor follows the new first line.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
